1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:02,360
Clap your hands inside a cathedral and the

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:06,150
sound doesn't stop. It rings, it echoes, it

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:09,170
slowly fades. Now play a whole song in

4
00:00:09,170 --> 00:00:12,540
that cathedral. Every note gets its own echo,

5
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:14,500
and all those echoes pile on top of

6
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,960
each other. What reaches your ears is a

7
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:19,720
perfect blend of the song and the room.

8
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:23,060
That blending has a name, convolution, and it's

9
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:26,080
everywhere. It blurs the photos on your phone.

10
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:28,790
It helps neural network see edges. It tells

11
00:00:28,790 --> 00:00:30,920
you the odds when you roll two dice.

12
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:33,860
It's even hiding inside the long multiplication you

13
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:38,040
learned in school. One simple recipe, flip, slide,

14
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:41,150
multiply, and add. In the next few minutes,

15
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:43,990
we'll build convolution from scratch using nothing but

16
00:00:43,990 --> 00:00:46,630
shapes sliding past each other. And at the

17
00:00:46,630 --> 00:00:49,210
end, we'll see why the Fourier transform turns

18
00:00:49,210 --> 00:00:53,080
all that sliding into a single, simple multiplication.

19
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:55,260
Let's start with something you can do by

20
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:58,080
hand. One a week of temperatures that jump

21
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:02,150
around, 20, 24, 18, 26. To smooth them

22
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:04,440
out, take three days at a time, multiply

23
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,120
each by one third, and add them up.

24
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:10,130
Then slide one day forward and repeat. That

25
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:12,960
little window of weights, one third, one third,

26
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:15,530
one third, is called a kernel. And sliding

27
00:01:15,530 --> 00:01:18,030
a kernel along a signal, multiplying and adding

28
00:01:18,030 --> 00:01:21,560
at every position, is exactly what convolution is.

29
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:24,450
Let's make it even smaller. Here's a signal

30
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:27,770
with just three numbers, one, two, three. And

31
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:30,660
here is a kernel with two numbers, one,

32
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:33,650
one. First we flip the kernel around. Then

33
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:35,570
we slide it in from the left, one

34
00:01:35,570 --> 00:01:38,080
step at a time. At the first position,

35
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:41,180
only one pair overlaps. One times one gives

36
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:44,970
one. Slide again. Now two pairs overlap. One

37
00:01:44,970 --> 00:01:47,880
times one plus two times one gives three.

38
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:51,100
Slide again. Two times one plus three times

39
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:54,020
one gives five. And one last time, and

40
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:56,810
only the three is left, three. So the

41
00:01:56,810 --> 00:02:00,730
answer is one, three, five, three. Now here's

42
00:02:00,730 --> 00:02:04,980
the surprise. Multiply 123 by 11. You get

43
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:10,960
1,353, one, three, five, three. The same digits.

44
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:14,360
Long multiplication is convolution, sliding one list of

45
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:17,840
digits across another. But why flip the kernel?

46
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:20,190
Think of the echo. What you hear right

47
00:02:20,190 --> 00:02:22,670
now is the sound arriving this instant, plus

48
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:24,910
a quieter copy from a moment ago, plus

49
00:02:24,910 --> 00:02:27,670
a fainter copy from before that. The output

50
00:02:27,670 --> 00:02:29,780
looks backwards in time, and the flip is

51
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:31,700
how we line up the present with the

52
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:34,560
past. Now let's roll some dice. A fair

53
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,560
die gives each face a one in six

54
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:39,450
chance, six bars of equal height, a flat

55
00:02:39,450 --> 00:02:42,380
shape. Roll two dice and add them. A

56
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:45,240
total of two can happen only one way.

57
00:02:45,270 --> 00:02:47,900
A total of seven can happen six ways,

58
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:50,820
more than any other. Then the count falls

59
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:53,080
back to a single way to make twelve.

60
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:55,970
Watch it as convolution. Flip one row of

61
00:02:55,970 --> 00:02:58,560
six bars and slide it across the other.

62
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:02,240
At first, only one bar overlaps. The overlap

63
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:05,360
grows until at seven all six line up.

64
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:08,120
Then it shrinks again. The flat shape has

65
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:10,700
become a triangle. Add a third die and

66
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:13,770
convolve again, and the triangle softens into a

67
00:03:13,770 --> 00:03:16,810
hill. Keep adding dice and the hill becomes

68
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:20,100
a smooth bell. That's the central limit theorem,

69
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:23,410
seen as convolution repeated again and again. Now

70
00:03:23,410 --> 00:03:26,390
let's make our shapes smooth. When our signals

71
00:03:26,390 --> 00:03:29,840
become continuous curves, the sum becomes an integral.

72
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:31,960
Here is the formula. F star g of

73
00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:35,270
t equals the integral from minus infinity to

74
00:03:35,270 --> 00:03:37,560
infinity of f of tau times g of

75
00:03:37,560 --> 00:03:41,400
t minus tau d tau. It looks intimidating,

76
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:43,650
but read it in pieces. g of minus

77
00:03:43,650 --> 00:03:46,530
tau is just g flipped around. g of

78
00:03:46,530 --> 00:03:49,200
t minus tau is that flipped copy slid

79
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:52,180
over to position t. We multiply it by

80
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:55,160
f point by point. And the integral measures

81
00:03:55,160 --> 00:03:57,470
the total area of the overlap. So the

82
00:03:57,470 --> 00:04:00,740
convolution at time t is simply this. Flip

83
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:03,120
one shape, slide it to t, and measure

84
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:06,370
the overlap. Let's try the classic example. Two

85
00:04:06,370 --> 00:04:09,160
boxes, each of height one and width one.

86
00:04:09,190 --> 00:04:12,150
Now apart, there's no overlap, so the output

87
00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:14,500
is zero. Slide one into the other, and

88
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:16,940
the overlap grows in a straight line. When

89
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:19,640
they line up perfectly, it peaks at one.

90
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:22,410
Then it shrinks back to zero. Two boxes

91
00:04:22,410 --> 00:04:26,740
have made a triangle. Convolution's smooths, spreads, and

92
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:30,030
blends. Now let's step into two dimensions. An

93
00:04:30,030 --> 00:04:32,740
image is a grid of brightness values. Our

94
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:35,350
kernel becomes a small grid two, three by

95
00:04:35,350 --> 00:04:37,930
three. Place it over a patch of pixels,

96
00:04:37,930 --> 00:04:40,200
multiply each pixel by the weight above it,

97
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:42,680
add all nine, and write the result into

98
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:45,650
a new image. Then slide one pixel over,

99
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:48,000
and repeat. Fill the kernel with one ninth

100
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,550
everywhere, and each pixel becomes the average of

101
00:04:50,550 --> 00:04:54,090
its neighbors, the image blurs. Now put minus

102
00:04:54,090 --> 00:04:56,150
one on the left column, zero in the

103
00:04:56,150 --> 00:04:58,550
middle, and plus one on the right. In

104
00:04:58,550 --> 00:05:01,440
flat areas, left and right cancel to zero.

105
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:04,710
But wherever brightness suddenly changes, the kernel lights

106
00:05:04,710 --> 00:05:07,740
up. We've built an edge detector, and here's

107
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:11,600
the remarkable part. Convolutional neural networks aren't told

108
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,910
which kernels to use. They learn them from

109
00:05:13,910 --> 00:05:16,870
millions of examples, and their very first layers

110
00:05:16,870 --> 00:05:19,770
discover edge detectors almost exactly like the one

111
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:22,710
we just built by hand. There's just one

112
00:05:22,710 --> 00:05:27,020
problem, sliding is slow. Convolving a million samples

113
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:29,670
with a million samples takes around a trillion

114
00:05:29,670 --> 00:05:33,360
multiplications. This is where the Fourier transform comes

115
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:35,810
to the rescue. Add a single pure tone

116
00:05:35,810 --> 00:05:38,740
into the cathedral. Every echo is a copy

117
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:41,960
of that same tone, just delayed and quieter.

118
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:44,380
And adding copies of one sine wave always

119
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:46,860
gives back the same sine wave, only its

120
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:50,410
size and timing change. So every frequency passes

121
00:05:50,410 --> 00:05:54,240
through a convolution untouched, just scaled and shifted.

122
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:57,100
We can split any signal into frequencies, scale

123
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:00,210
each one, and put everything back together. That

124
00:06:00,210 --> 00:06:03,340
is the convolution theorem. The Fourier transform of

125
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:05,550
f star g equals the Fourier transform of

126
00:06:05,550 --> 00:06:09,450
f times the Fourier transform of g. Convolution

127
00:06:09,450 --> 00:06:12,570
in time becomes simple multiplication in frequency. With

128
00:06:12,570 --> 00:06:15,800
the fast Fourier transform, that trillion multiplications drops

129
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:19,220
to roughly 100 million. This is how audio

130
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:22,120
software adds reverb in real time. Record a

131
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:24,110
single clap in a real cathedral, and that

132
00:06:24,110 --> 00:06:27,580
recording is the room's impulse response. Hold any

133
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:29,550
song with it, and the song sounds as

134
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:32,190
if it were played inside that cathedral. And

135
00:06:32,190 --> 00:06:34,840
the same idea lives inside the Laplace transform

136
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:37,540
and the Z-transform. Every time an engineer writes

137
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:39,290
that the output equals the input times a

138
00:06:39,290 --> 00:06:42,760
transfer function, they are quietly using the convolution

139
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:45,170
theorem. So let's look back at what we

140
00:06:45,170 --> 00:06:49,590
discovered. Convolution is one simple recipe. Flip, slide,

141
00:06:49,590 --> 00:06:52,740
multiply, and add. With lists of numbers, it

142
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:55,310
smooths noisy data, and it is the hidden

143
00:06:55,310 --> 00:06:58,590
engine behind long multiplication. With dice, it turns

144
00:06:58,590 --> 00:07:01,410
flat chances into triangles, and then into the

145
00:07:01,410 --> 00:07:04,570
bell curve. With continuous curves, it measures the

146
00:07:04,570 --> 00:07:06,880
area of overlap as one shape slides across

147
00:07:06,880 --> 00:07:10,670
another. With images, small kernels blur, sharpen, and

148
00:07:10,670 --> 00:07:13,860
find edges, and neural networks learn those kernels

149
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:16,730
on their own. And through the Fourier transform,

150
00:07:16,730 --> 00:07:18,880
all of that sliding collapses into a single

151
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:22,700
multiplication, frequency by frequency. The next time you

152
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:24,670
hear an echo, see a blurry photo, or

153
00:07:24,670 --> 00:07:26,530
roll a pair of dice, you'll know the

154
00:07:26,530 --> 00:07:29,660
same quiet operation is at work underneath. If

155
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:32,460
this helped convolution finally click, subscribe, and I'll

156
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:33,680
see you in the next one.

